verdict = {
  H_SEN1_null:  "CONFIRMED — a stable kernel at constant conditions does NOT senesce internally. The
                 seed's H_null branch materialised: 'senescence requires open-system coupling, not
                 internal drift' (Beer: viable system ↔ environment).",
  H_SEN2_drift: "TESTED (run_C transition, 2026-06-23, results/run_C_transition.log, 45k ticks). One
                 calibration, then a STEP load (2×nproc `yes`) applied post-warmup; drift measured FROM
                 the single frozen baseline. Result: drift BOUNDED — plateaus at ~0.017–0.019 over ticks
                 20k–45k (oscillating, NOT monotone runaway); stasis_cos → 1.00000 (viability preserved,
                 improving). ⇒ sustained load does NOT induce senescence; bounded homeostatic adaptation.
                 CAVEAT: baseline contaminated — the n02 host (4 cores) was structurally loaded (ollama S4
                 inference + python timers ≈ load 8) during warmup, so the kernel's CPU-pressure read was
                 elevated and h_rest_ref froze at 1.83384 (≈ the LOAD set-point) instead of true idle 1.79.
                 So run_C is a 'loaded-host-idle → +step-load' excursion, not a clean 'true-idle → load' one.
                 The bounded-vs-runaway CONCLUSION is baseline-independent and holds; the clean true-idle→
                 escalating-ramp excursion is deferred to a quiet-host window or the BBH external-stressor
                 bridge (EXP-L1-01). Root cause = warmup snapshots ref ~420 stasis-ticks in, catching the
                 canonical-stack startup transient under host contention; a min-idle-ticks gate would make
                 calibration deterministic (env-gated, byte-identical-prod-safe) — noted for follow-up.",
  key_result:   "the set-point SHIFT (1.79→1.84 under load) is the real measured cross-regime signal —
                 the homeostatic response, distinct from senescence.",
}

